course: dictionary
lesson: 3868
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Frame is one of them. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning fits. «A triangle frame keeps its shape when pushed, but a square frame can be pushed out of shape.» Here frame means a strong structure that holds something up, because shape and pushed are about things that are built.
explain: «A coin that falls 2, then 4, then 6 pixels in three frames has fallen 2 + 4 + 6 = 12 pixels.» Here frame means one picture in a film, because the coin moves a little in each picture. Ask yourself: which meaning would make sense in this sentence? Then test it by putting your meaning in place of the word.
explain: Remember that a word can change its job. Frame can be a noun, a thing, or a verb, an action. When you frame a picture, you put it into a frame. The same word can have a picture border, a body shape and a made-up crime all at once, so always check the clues.
fact: Some words have several meanings, so always check the context.
fact: «Frame» can mean a border around something, like a picture frame or a window frame.
fact: «Frame» can mean the strong structure that holds something up, like a bike frame.
fact: «Frame» can mean one picture in a film or comic, and «to frame someone» means to make them look guilty when they are not.
exercise: «A triangle frame keeps its shape when pushed.» In this sentence, what does frame mean? | One picture in a film | *The strong structure that holds something up | A border around a picture | The size and shape of your body
exercise: «A coin that falls 2, then 4, then 6 pixels in three frames has fallen 12 pixels.» What is a frame in this sentence? | A border around a picture | Your body size | A bar on a climbing frame | *One picture in a film
exercise: Ava says, «I will frame my drawing so it looks nice on the wall.» What does frame mean here? | Make it look guilty | Draw a new picture | Count the pixels | *Put it in a border
test: «Glasses frames are light, so they sit comfortably on your nose.» What does frame mean? | To make someone look guilty | *The part that holds the lenses | The size of your body | A picture in a film
test: «The detective found that he had been framed.» What does framed mean? | Held up by a strong structure | Put inside a picture border | *Made to look guilty when he was not | Counted in pixels
exam: Which meaning of frame fits when you measure the wood that goes around the edge of a square? | One picture in a film | Your body shape | *A border around the edge | To make someone look guilty
